//? Title: SLR: actual LR construction and parse stacks
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A token sequence must be recognized against a context-free grammar, including
natural left recursion. SLR builds LR(0) item states and allows reductions only
on the completed rule's FOLLOW terminals. We inspect actual constructed
sets/actions and executed stacks, retain the original expression grammar, and
reject unsafe conflicted-table parsing.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

An item A -> alpha dot beta records a recognized prefix. Closure adds every B ->
dot gamma when the dot precedes nonterminal B. GOTO advances the dot over one
symbol and closes the result. These actual sets form the deterministic
viable-prefix automaton.

A terminal transition produces shift; a completed A -> alpha reduces only for
lookaheads in FOLLOW(A). Reducing pops |alpha| states and follows GOTO for A;
epsilon pops zero. The synthetic completed start accepts at the sole appended
end marker. A grammar is SLR(1) only if no ACTION key requires different
actions. FIRST/FOLLOW are fixed points, not single-pass approximations.

The state stack denotes a viable prefix. Shift extends it by a terminal and
reduction replaces a recognized handle by its nonterminal, so an accept at the
end recognizes the complete input. The trace below comes from these actual
lookups. SLR is a restricted LR construction; an arbitrary LR grammar need not
be SLR, and grammar-class comparisons with all LL(k) languages require care.
There is no claim that all top-down parser techniques fail on every
left-recursive grammar transformation.

LR state counts can grow exponentially with grammar size. Explicit ordered sets
and repeated closures are bounded separately from parse actions; captured whole
stacks can be much larger than the token stream. Nullable cycles and bad
grammars cannot run without an explicit action limit.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/slr_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    a::ContextFreeGrammar grammar{{{"S", {"id"}}}, "S"};
    a::SLRParser parser(grammar);
    auto r = parser.parse_trace({"id"});
    std::print("| consumed | actual lookahead | action | value | actual state stack |\n| ---: | --- | --- | " "---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &s : r.steps)
        std::print("| {} | `{}` | {} | {} | `{}` |\n", s.input_offset,
                   s.lookahead, s.action, s.value, s.states);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Grammar S -> id; one input token");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual LR states", parser.state_count()));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Accepted {}, {} lookups", r.accepted,
                                     r.actions));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/slr_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| consumed | actual lookahead | action | value | actual state stack |
| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | --- |
| 1 | `id` | shift | 2 | `[0, 2]` |
| 1 | `$` | reduce | 1 | `[0, 1]` |
| 1 | `$` | accept | 0 | `[0, 1]` |
